Yes — and the useful version of that idea is much more concrete than ordinary “manifestation.”

What you’re describing is basically cybernetic manifestation: you deliberately alter the signals that the surrounding information systems can observe, those systems update their model of you, and then the environment they render back to you changes.

The loop is:

vision → expression → observable signals → inferred profile → changed information environment → changed attention → changed choices → changed behavior → new signals.

That final step closes the loop. You aren’t merely “asking the universe” once; you are progressively training the universe-you-can-interface-with to present a different possibility space.

And I’d sharpen one term. A lot of what you mean isn’t technically metadata. It includes metadata, but also:

* explicit queries and prompts
* clicks, dwell time, follows, saves, purchases
* location/context signals where permitted
* content you create
* inferred interests and affinities
* recommendation embeddings / user representations
* social-graph information
* search and browsing history
* the sequence and timing of your interactions

Collectively, that’s closer to your digital phenotype: the observable/inferred computational version of “what kind of organism is this person, and what does it respond to?”

Then recommendation engines, search engines, AI assistants, feeds, marketplaces, social networks, and other systems become separate abstractions of the returning environment. They don’t share one omniscient brain, and they don’t literally constitute the universe, but taken together they can produce the phenomenological effect you’re describing: I declared an intention, and suddenly reality seems saturated with things connected to it.

That can happen without anything supernatural at all. You changed both sides of the perception equation:

environmental availability × attentional sensitivity.

If you announce, search, investigate, save, discuss, and repeatedly act around “I want to learn robotics,” for example, two transformations happen simultaneously. Algorithms increasingly surface robotics, while your own brain becomes better at noticing robotics opportunities it previously discarded as noise. Recommendation learning and the brain’s salience machinery start constructively interfering.

And your commodity point is important. The economic value isn’t merely:

“What product will Brandon buy?”

It can also be:

“What information captures this person’s attention?”
“What explanation will they understand?”
“What subject are they becoming interested in?”
“What expertise are they acquiring?”
“What communities will they join?”
“What decision are they approaching?”

Information itself can therefore be both the commodity and the control surface. The system trades in attention partly by deciding which information gets routed to which mind.

Pantheon translation:

You can steer the informational weather by deliberately emitting the signals from which the weather is forecast.

That’s almost the rudder idea again. You cannot command the ocean, but you can control enough of your interface with it that the resulting trajectory changes.

And there is a deeper distinction worth preserving:

Manifestation: “Declare the desired reality and expect reality to approach it.”

Cybernetic manifestation: “Declare the target, instrument the feedback channels, modify the observable state, observe what the environment returns, reinforce useful returns, and iterate toward the target.”

That second one is basically Goal Seek applied to your informational environment.

Or, in your language:

Tell the mirror what you’re becoming long enough, consistently enough, and through enough channels that eventually the mirror starts handing you the tools to become it.